<file path="docs/ARCHITECTURE.md">
# NVIDIA Sionna RT MCP Server — Architecture Documentation

**Document Version:** 1.0  
**Target Platform:** Windows 11 64-bit  
**Runtime:** CPython 3.10.10, Sionna RT 2.2.0, Mitsuba 3.9.1, Dr.Jit 1.5.0, LLVM 18.1.6  
**Protocol:** Model Context Protocol (MCP) 1.30.0 over stdio transport  

---

## 1. Architectural Philosophy and Design Principles

The `sionna_rt_mcp` project provides an agentic bridge between AI clients (such as Claude Desktop, Cursor, or autonomous LLM agents) and NVIDIA Sionna RT, a state-of-the-art differentiable ray-tracing engine for wireless communications.

The architecture adheres strictly to the following core software engineering principles:

1. **Strict Layered Separation of Concerns:**
   - The **MCP Server Layer** (`src/mcp_server.py`) acts solely as an interface adapter. It handles JSON-RPC message serialization, argument schema validation, tool registration, and error wrapping. It contains **no ray-tracing mathematics, scene-graph manipulation, or spatial comparison logic**.
   - The **Simulation Engine Layer** (`src/engine.py`, `src/radio_map_engine.py`) encapsulates all interactions with Sionna RT and Mitsuba 3.
   - The **Domain Model Layer** (`src/config.py`, `src/result.py`, `src/radio_map_config.py`, `src/radio_map_result.py`) provides strongly typed, immutable dataclasses.
   - The **Persistence Layer** (`src/experiment_store.py`) manages filesystem persistence, atomic writes, and pickle-free deserialization.
   - The **Spatial Analysis Layer** (`src/experiment_comparator.py`) performs numerical comparisons, difference masking, and statistical evaluation.

2. **Stateless Server with Stateful Filesystem Persistence:**
   - The MCP server process maintains no in-memory experiment cache across tool calls. Every query or comparison operates directly against validated filesystem artifacts managed by `ExperimentStore`. This guarantees process stability and crash immunity.

3. **Defensive Physical Plausibility & Numeric Integrity:**
   - RF power is strictly enforced in Watts ($W$) and dBm ($P_{\text{dBm}} = 10 \log_{10}(P_W) + 30$).
   - Path gains are strictly non-negative linear values converted to dB ($G_{\text{dB}} = 10 \log_{10}(G)$).
   - Zero-power / unreachable cells strictly evaluate to $-\infty$ (IEEE 754 `-inf`). The system never substitutes artificial RF noise floors for unreached cells.
   - Unreached cells are masked transparently in heatmaps and cleanly omitted from comparison statistics.

4. **Canonical Import Identity & Single Type Registry:**
   - Internal modules use canonical package imports (`from src.<module> import ...`) with fallback to local imports.
   - A runtime harmonization hook (`_harmonize_module_aliases`) binds module names in `sys.modules`, ensuring strict type identity and eliminating fragile class-name introspection fallbacks.

---

## 2. End-to-End System Architecture

The following Mermaid diagram illustrates the actual runtime architecture, data flows, and subsystem boundaries across the entire codebase:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph ClientLayer["AI Client Layer"]
        Client["MCP Client\n(Claude Desktop / Cursor / Scripts)"]
    end

    subgraph TransportLayer["Transport & Protocol Layer"]
        Transport["stdio Transport\n(JSON-RPC 2.0 / FastMCP)"]
    end

    subgraph ServerLayer["MCP Server Layer (src/mcp_server.py)"]
        Server["FastMCP Server Instance\n'sionna-rt-mcp'"]
        Harmonizer["_harmonize_module_aliases()\n(sys.modules unifier)"]
        
        subgraph ToolRegistry["Registered Tool Handlers"]
            T1["check_environment()"]
            T2["list_scenes()"]
            T3["run_simulation()"]
            T4["generate_radio_map()"]
            T5["list_experiments()"]
            T6["get_experiment_details()"]
            T7["compare_experiments()"]
        end
    end

    subgraph CoreEngine["Core Simulation Engines"]
        P2PEngine["SimulationEngine\n(src/engine.py)"]
        RMEngine["RadioMapEngine\n(src/radio_map_engine.py)"]
        P2PConfig["SimulationConfig\n(src/config.py)"]
        RMConfig["RadioMapConfig\n(src/radio_map_config.py)"]
    end

    subgraph SionnaStack["Physical Simulation Stack"]
        Sionna["NVIDIA Sionna RT 2.2.0"]
        PathSolver["PathSolver\n(SBR Ray Tracer)"]
        RMSolver["RadioMapSolver\n(Planar Coverage Solver)"]
        Mitsuba["Mitsuba 3.9.1 / Dr.Jit 1.5.0\n(llvm_ad_mono_polarized)"]
        LLVM["Isolated Standalone LLVM-C.dll\n(LLVM 18.1.6 CPU JIT)"]
    end

    subgraph ProcessingLayer["Result Processing & Visualization"]
        P2PResult["SimulationResult\n(src/result.py)"]
        RMResult["RadioMapResult\n(src/radio_map_result.py)"]
        RMFormatter["RadioMapFormatter\n(src/radio_map_formatter.py)"]
        Comparator["ExperimentComparator\n(src/experiment_comparator.py)"]
        CompResult["ComparisonResult\n(B - A Convention)"]
    end

    subgraph StorageLayer["Persistence Layer (experiments/)"]
        Store["ExperimentStore\n(src/experiment_store.py)"]
        MetaJSON["metadata.json\n(Config, Metrics, Artifacts)"]
        DataNPZ["radio_map_data.npz\n(Compressed Float64 Arrays)"]
        HeatmapsPNG["*.png Heatmaps\n(RSS, Path Gain, Diff)"]
    end

    %% Client and transport connections
    Client <-->|JSON-RPC Stdio| Transport
    Transport <--> Server
    Server --> Harmonizer
    Server --> ToolRegistry

    %% Tool routing
    T1 -.->|inspects| P2PEngine
    T2 -.->|inspects| Sionna
    T3 -->|configures| P2PConfig
    P2PConfig --> P2PEngine
    T4 -->|configures| RMConfig
    RMConfig --> RMEngine

    %% Simulation execution
    P2PEngine -->|scene, tx, rx| PathSolver
    RMEngine -->|scene, tx, rx_region| RMSolver
    PathSolver --> Mitsuba
    RMSolver --> Mitsuba
    Mitsuba --> LLVM

    %% Result extraction
    PathSolver --> P2PResult
    RMSolver --> RMResult
    RMResult --> RMFormatter
    RMFormatter --> HeatmapsPNG

    %% Storage operations
    T4 --> Store
    RMResult --> Store
    Store --> MetaJSON
    Store --> DataNPZ
    Store --> HeatmapsPNG

    %% Experiment inspection & comparison
    T5 --> Store
    T6 --> Store
    T7 --> Comparator
    Store -->|loads A & B| Comparator
    Comparator --> CompResult
    Comparator --> HeatmapsPNG

    %% Return to Client
    P2PResult -->|serialized JSON| Server
    RMResult -->|summary JSON| Server
    CompResult -->|comparison JSON| Server
    Store -->|metadata JSON| Server
```

---

## 3. Subsystem Breakdown and Responsibilities

### 3.1 Point-to-Point Ray Tracing Subsystem

The point-to-point subsystem simulates radio propagation between a single transmitter and a single receiver node in a 3D geometric scene.

- **`src/config.py` (`SimulationConfig`):**
  Defines carrier frequency (`frequency_hz`, default 3.5 GHz), scene identifier (`scene_name`), transmitter position (`tx_position`, default `[0.0, -10.0, 5.0]`), transmit power (`tx_power_dbm`, default 30.0 dBm), receiver position (`rx_position`, default `[0.0, 10.0, 5.0]`), and planar array antenna specifications (`pattern="iso"`, `polarization="V"`).
- **`src/engine.py` (`SimulationEngine`):**
  - Manages backend initialization via `ensure_mitsuba_variant()`.
  - Loads 3D meshes from `sionna.rt.scene` or filesystem XML.
  - Instantiates `PlanarArray`, `Transmitter`, and `Receiver`. Aligns transmitter towards receiver via `tx.look_at(rx)`.
  - Executes Sionna RT `PathSolver()`.
  - Extracts path amplitudes ($a \in \mathbb{C}$), delays ($\tau$), propagation distances ($d = \tau \cdot c$), individual path gains, total path loss, and interaction types (Line-of-Sight, Specular Reflection, Diffuse Scattering, Diffraction).
- **`src/result.py` (`SimulationResult`):**
  Immutable dataclass encapsulating all propagation metrics with property accessors and a full `to_dict()` JSON serialization method.
- **`src/formatter.py` (`format_result`):**
  Terminal formatter presenting physical propagation metrics in clear, human-readable ASCII tables.

---

### 3.2 Radio-Map Coverage Subsystem

The radio-map subsystem computes 2D spatial wireless coverage across continuous planar evaluation surfaces.

- **`src/radio_map_config.py` (`RadioMapConfig`):**
  - Comprehensive configuration dataclass validating all parameters before simulation.
  - Supports **AUTO** region bounding (dynamically extracted from scene geometry with configurable margin and transmitter inclusion) and **MANUAL** bounding ($x_{\min}, x_{\max}, y_{\min}, y_{\max}$).
  - Controls evaluation height (`receiver_height`, default 1.5 m) and spatial resolution (`cell_size`, scalar or $[dx, dy]$).
  - Configures ray budget (`samples_per_tx`), bounce recursion depth (`max_depth`), random seed, and ray types (LOS, reflections, diffraction, scattering).
  - Maps user-friendly antenna categories (`"isotropic"`, `"dipole"`, `"directional"`) to verified Sionna RT PlanarArray patterns (`"iso"`, `"dipole"`, `"tr38901"`).
- **`src/radio_map_engine.py` (`RadioMapEngine`):**
  - Instantiates transmitter antenna array, orientation, and power.
  - Computes receiver measurement plane bounding box and center coordinate.
  - Configures and executes Sionna RT's native `RadioMapSolver`.
  - Preserves spatial bounds and coordinate properties for downstream extractors.
- **`src/radio_map_result.py` (`RadioMapResult`):**
  - Extracts 2D linear received power (`rss_watts`), path gain (`path_gain_linear`), and 3D cell centers (`cell_centers`).
  - Converts linear fields to logarithmic representations:
    $$\text{RSS}_{\text{dBm}} = 10 \log_{10}(P_{\text{watts}}) + 30$$
    $$\text{PathGain}_{\text{dB}} = 10 \log_{10}(G_{\text{linear}})$$
  - Converts zero-power / unreached cells directly to $-\infty$ (`-inf`) without precision loss or synthetic noise floor injection.
  - Automatically derives 1D separable or 2D grid coordinates from cell centers upon instantiation.
- **`src/radio_map_formatter.py` (`RadioMapFormatter`):**
  - Renders 150 DPI publication-quality heatmaps for Received Signal Strength (`plot_received_power_heatmap`) and Path Gain (`plot_path_gain_heatmap`).
  - Utilizes Matplotlib's `Agg` headless backend.
  - Masks unreachable cells ($-\infty$) transparently using `np.ma.masked_where` and colors them with a neutral light gray (`#dcdcdc`).

---

### 3.3 Experiment Persistence and Comparison Subsystem

- **`src/experiment_store.py` (`ExperimentStore`):**
  - Manages atomic filesystem storage under `experiments/experiment_<timestamp>_<uuid>/`.
  - Atomic write strategy: Files are prepared in temporary staging folders (`.tmp_*`) and renamed to the final destination in a single atomic OS filesystem operation.
  - Numerical arrays are stored in compressed NPZ (`radio_map_data.npz`) using strict `float64` typing.
  - Deserialization enforces `allow_pickle=False` to eliminate arbitrary code execution vulnerabilities.
  - Validates all experiment IDs against path traversal patterns (`../`, `..\`, `/`).
- **`src/experiment_comparator.py` (`ExperimentComparator`):**
  - Enforces spatial compatibility across 7 criteria: scene name, receiver height, grid dimensions, cell size, region bounds, cell centers, and coordinate vectors.
  - Implements the strict **$B - A$ (Experiment B minus Experiment A)** convention:
    $$\Delta \text{RSS}_{\text{dBm}} = \text{RSS}_B - \text{RSS}_A$$
    $$\Delta \text{PathGain}_{\text{dB}} = \text{PathGain}_B - \text{PathGain}_A$$
  - Unreached cells in either experiment are masked as `NaN` in delta matrices and excluded from statistical metrics.
  - Renders centered diverging difference heatmaps (`PuOr` colormap) with neutral gray masking for invalid cells.

---

### 3.4 MCP Server Interface Layer

- **`src/mcp_server.py`:**
  - Built with the official Python Model Context Protocol SDK (`mcp==1.30.0`, `FastMCP`).
  - Exposes all 7 tools over standard I/O (`stdio` JSON-RPC transport).
  - Performs schema validation using Pydantic field annotations.
  - Maps internal exceptions (`ValueError`, `FileNotFoundError`, `RuntimeError`) to formal MCP `ToolError` instances with descriptive user-facing diagnostics.
  - Provides `_harmonize_module_aliases()` to ensure consistent module identity in `sys.modules`.
</file>

<file path="docs/EXPERIMENT_STORAGE.md">
# Experiment Storage Architecture and File Artifacts Specification

This document details the filesystem-based persistence architecture, serialization schemas, naming conventions, and integrity safeguards implemented in `src/experiment_store.py`.

---

## 1. Storage Overview and Directory Hierarchy

All experiments are stored locally in the `experiments/` directory located at the project root. The storage architecture is entirely file-based, requiring no external database or server dependencies.

### Directory Structure:

```text
experiments/
├── comparisons/                                             # Directory storing spatial difference heatmaps
│   ├── diff_experiment_<idA>_vs_experiment_<idB>_rss_db.png       # Diverging RSS difference heatmap
│   └── diff_experiment_<idA>_vs_experiment_<idB>_path_gain_db.png # Diverging Path Gain difference heatmap
│
├── experiment_20261101_135328_38565204/                     # Single experiment directory
│   ├── metadata.json                                        # Complete simulation metadata and summary metrics
│   ├── radio_map_data.npz                                   # Compressed NumPy arrays (float64, pickle-free)
│   ├── rss_heatmap.png                                      # Received Signal Strength (dBm) heatmap
│   └── path_gain_heatmap.png                                # Path Gain (dB) heatmap
│
└── experiment_20261101_135328_4b634b84/
    ├── metadata.json
    ├── radio_map_data.npz
    ├── rss_heatmap.png
    └── path_gain_heatmap.png
```

---

## 2. Experiment Identification Scheme

Every experiment is assigned a unique identifier following the canonical format:

$$\text{experiment\_}\langle\text{YYYYMMDD}\rangle\text{\_}\langle\text{HHMMSS}\rangle\text{\_}\langle\text{uuid8}\rangle$$

- **Timestamp Component:** UTC timestamp formatted as `YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS` ensures chronological sorting on filesystem directories.
- **UUID Component:** 8 hexadecimal characters generated from `uuid.uuid4().hex[:8]` guarantees uniqueness even under concurrent generation.
- **Example:** `experiment_20261101_135328_38565204`
- **Validation:** Strict regex validation `^[a-zA-Z0-9_\-]+$` rejects any path traversal characters (`..`, `/`, `\`).

---

## 3. Detailed Artifact Specifications

### 3.1 `metadata.json`

The `metadata.json` file records all input configurations, runtime environment attributes, array shapes, and statistical summary metrics. It enables lightweight inspection without loading numerical arrays.

#### JSON Schema Structure:

```json
{
  "schema_version": "1.0",
  "experiment_id": "experiment_20261101_135328_38565204",
  "experiment_name": "validation_run_30dbm",
  "created_at": "2026-11-01T13:53:28.159443+00:00",
  "simulation_timestamp": "2026-11-01T13:53:27.736018+00:00",
  "scene_name": "simple_reflector",
  "frequency_hz": 3500000000.0,
  "tx_position": [0.0, -10.0, 5.0],
  "tx_power_dbm": 30.0,
  "tx_orientation_deg": [0.0, 0.0, 0.0],
  "antenna_type": "isotropic",
  "antenna_pattern": "iso",
  "polarization": "V",
  "measurement_height": 1.5,
  "region_bounds": [-5.5, 5.5, -15.0, 5.5],
  "cell_size": [5.0, 5.0],
  "grid_dimensions": [5, 3],
  "solver_parameters": {
    "samples_per_tx": 10000,
    "max_depth": 3,
    "seed": null,
    "los": true,
    "specular_reflection": true,
    "diffraction": true,
    "scattering": false
  },
  "backend_variant": "llvm_ad_mono_polarized",
  "total_cells": 15,
  "valid_cells": 15,
  "zero_power_cells": 0,
  "coverage_percentage": 100.0,
  "min_rss_dbm": -37.9167,
  "mean_rss_dbm": -32.0853,
  "max_rss_dbm": -25.3994,
  "artifact_paths": {
    "rss_heatmap": "C:\\...\\experiments\\experiment_...\\rss_heatmap.png",
    "path_gain_heatmap": "C:\\...\\experiments\\experiment_...\\path_gain_heatmap.png",
    "metadata_json": "C:\\...\\experiments\\experiment_...\\metadata.json",
    "radio_map_data_npz": "C:\\...\\experiments\\experiment_...\\radio_map_data.npz"
  },
  "heatmaps_available": {
    "rss_heatmap": true,
    "path_gain_heatmap": true
  }
}
```

---

### 3.2 `radio_map_data.npz`

The `radio_map_data.npz` file stores complete, uncompressed or compressed raw numerical tensors.

#### Saved Arrays:

| Array Key | Data Type | Dimensions | Description | Handling of Zero-Power Cells |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- | :--- |
| `path_gain_linear` | `float64` | `[Ny, Nx]` | Linear power transfer gain ($G_{\text{lin}} \ge 0.0$) | Exactly `0.0` |
| `path_gain_db` | `float64` | `[Ny, Nx]` | Logarithmic path gain ($10 \log_{10}(G)$) | Exactly `-inf` ($-\infty$) |
| `rss_watts` | `float64` | `[Ny, Nx]` | Received RF power in Watts ($P_W \ge 0.0$) | Exactly `0.0` |
| `rss_dbm` | `float64` | `[Ny, Nx]` | Received Signal Strength ($10 \log_{10}(P) + 30$) | Exactly `-inf` ($-\infty$) |
| `cell_centers` | `float64` | `[Ny, Nx, 3]` | 3D Cartesian coordinates $[x, y, z]$ per cell center | Actual spatial coordinate $(x, y, z)$ |
| `x_coordinates` | `float64` | `[Nx]` or `[Ny, Nx]` | X-axis coordinate vector or 2D coordinate mesh | Real float coordinate |
| `y_coordinates` | `float64` | `[Ny]` or `[Ny, Nx]` | Y-axis coordinate vector or 2D coordinate mesh | Real float coordinate |

#### Pickling Prohibition:
All arrays are cast to `np.float64` before writing. Deserialization strictly uses `np.load(npz_path, allow_pickle=False)`. This guarantees safety and prevents arbitrary code execution.

---

### 3.3 Visual Heatmap Artifacts (`*.png`)

1. **`rss_heatmap.png`:**
   - 2D spatial distribution of Received Signal Strength in dBm.
   - Rendered using the `viridis` colormap.
   - Transmitter location marked with a red star marker ($\star$).
   - Unreachable cells (zero power) are masked with a neutral light gray (`#dcdcdc`).
   - Resolution: 150 DPI (typically 65–85 KB).

2. **`path_gain_heatmap.png`:**
   - 2D spatial distribution of logarithmic Path Gain in dB.
   - Rendered using the `plasma` colormap.
   - Shows intrinsic channel propagation attenuation independent of transmitter power.

3. **Comparison Difference Heatmaps:**
   - Saved under `experiments/comparisons/`.
   - Naming: `diff_<idA>_vs_<idB>_rss_db.png` and `diff_<idA>_vs_<idB>_path_gain_db.png`.
   - Centered diverging colormap (`PuOr` / `RdBu_r`) where $0.0$ dB is neutral.
   - Positive regions ($> 0$ dB, Experiment B stronger) and negative regions ($< 0$ dB, Experiment B weaker) are clearly distinguished.
   - Invalid cells (unreached in either A or B) are rendered in neutral gray.

---

## 4. Integrity, Atomic Writes, and Safety Safeguards

### 4.1 Atomic Writes via Staging Directories
To prevent corrupted or half-written experiments caused by process crashes or disk interruptions:
1. All files (`metadata.json`, `radio_map_data.npz`, heatmaps) are written to a temporary staging folder:
   `experiments/.tmp_XXXXXX/`
2. Once all files are written and verified, the staging directory is renamed atomically to its final destination `experiments/experiment_<id>/`.
3. If an error occurs during writing, the staging folder is deleted, leaving no orphaned or broken directories.

### 4.2 Path Traversal Prevention
Any user-supplied or client-supplied `experiment_id` is sanitized using:
```python
if not re.match(r"^[a-zA-Z0-9_\-]+$", exp_id):
    raise ValueError(f"Invalid experiment_id '{exp_id}': contains illegal characters.")
```
Attempts to access `../` or absolute paths are immediately rejected.

### 4.3 Missing or Corrupted File Resilience
- If an experiment folder has a missing or malformed `metadata.json`, `ExperimentStore.list_experiments()` logs a warning and skips the entry without crashing.
- Direct queries (`get_experiment_details`) on corrupted experiments raise informative `ValueError` exceptions with exact diagnostics.
</file>

<file path="docs/MCP_TOOLS_REFERENCE.md">
# Model Context Protocol (MCP) Tools Reference

This document provides a comprehensive, rigorous API specification for all seven tools exposed by the `sionna_rt_mcp` server.

> [!NOTE]
> All seven tools are accessible identically across both **Streamable HTTP transport** (`http://127.0.0.1:8000/mcp`, started via `python server.py`) and standard **Stdio transport** (`python server.py --transport stdio` or `python src/mcp_server.py`). Tool schemas, arguments, physical calculations, and return structures are 100% identical across transports.

---

## Tool Summary Index

| # | Tool Name | Subsystem | Purpose | Primary Module Dependency |
| :-: | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **1** | `check_environment` | Diagnostic | Verify environment, software versions, and active backend | `src/engine.py` |
| **2** | `list_scenes` | Geometry | Catalog 3D propagation scenes available for simulation | `sionna.rt.scene` |
| **3** | `run_simulation` | Point-to-Point | Execute single TX-RX ray tracing and extract propagation metrics | `src/engine.py`, `src/config.py` |
| **4** | `generate_radio_map` | Coverage | Compute 2D spatial coverage map, render heatmaps, and persist | `src/radio_map_engine.py`, `src/experiment_store.py` |
| **5** | `list_experiments` | Storage | Retrieve lightweight metadata summaries of stored experiments | `src/experiment_store.py` |
| **6** | `get_experiment_details` | Storage | Retrieve comprehensive metadata and artifact paths for an experiment | `src/experiment_store.py` |
| **7** | `compare_experiments` | Spatial Analysis | Compute spatial differences ($B - A$) and render difference heatmaps | `src/experiment_comparator.py` |

---

## 1. `check_environment`

### Purpose
Probe whether Python, Sionna RT, and Mitsuba are properly installed and operational, identifying the active Mitsuba computational backend (e.g. CPU LLVM fallback or GPU OptiX). Call this tool before running simulations if environment availability is uncertain.

### Parameters
*None.* This tool takes no input arguments.

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `python_version` (`str`): CPython runtime version (e.g., `"3.10.10"`).
- `sionna_version` (`str`): Installed version of `sionna-rt` (e.g., `"2.2.0"`).
- `mitsuba_version` (`str`): Installed version of `mitsuba` (e.g., `"3.9.1"`).
- `backend` (`str`): Active Mitsuba backend variant (e.g., `"llvm_ad_mono_polarized"`).
- `is_usable` (`bool`): `true` if the simulation engine is operational; `false` otherwise.

### Sample Response
```json
{
  "python_version": "3.10.10",
  "sionna_version": "2.2.0",
  "mitsuba_version": "3.9.1",
  "backend": "llvm_ad_mono_polarized",
  "is_usable": true
}
```

### Errors Raised
- No exceptions are raised; failure to initialize the engine logs an error and sets `is_usable: false` and `backend: "unavailable"`.

---

## 2. `list_scenes`

### Purpose
Enumerate all 3D geometric propagation environments available to the simulation engine. Clients use this tool to discover valid `scene` or `scene_name` strings before running simulations.

### Parameters
*None.* This tool takes no input arguments.

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `scenes` (`list[dict]`): Catalog of scene descriptors, each containing:
  - `name` (`str`): Unique scene identifier (e.g., `"simple_reflector"`).
  - `description` (`str`): Technical description of the physical environment and expected propagation mechanisms.
  - `is_default` (`bool`): Whether this is the default scene.
- `default_scene` (`str`): Name of the canonical default scene (`"simple_reflector"`).
- `count` (`int`): Total count of registered scenes.

### Sample Response
```json
{
  "scenes": [
    {
      "name": "simple_reflector",
      "description": "Canonical two-path propagation scene containing a planar reflector. Produces a direct Line of Sight (LOS) ray and a single Specular Reflection ray.",
      "is_default": true
    }
  ],
  "default_scene": "simple_reflector",
  "count": 1
}
```

---

## 3. `run_simulation`

### Purpose
Execute an NVIDIA Sionna RT ray-tracing simulation between a point transmitter and a point receiver node in 3D space, resolving multipath components and extracting physical propagation metrics.

### Input Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Required? | Default | Constraints & Description |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| `scene` | `str` | Optional | `"simple_reflector"` | Geometric scene identifier. Must match a supported scene. |
| `frequency_hz` | `float` | Optional | `3.5e9` | Carrier frequency in **Hertz (Hz)** (e.g., `3.5e9` for 3.5 GHz). Must be $> 0.0$ Hz. |
| `tx_position` | `list[float]` | Optional | `[0.0, -10.0, 5.0]` | Transmitter Cartesian coordinates $[x, y, z]$ in meters (exactly 3 elements). |
| `rx_position` | `list[float]` | Optional | `[0.0, 10.0, 5.0]` | Receiver Cartesian coordinates $[x, y, z]$ in meters (exactly 3 elements). |
| `tx_power_dbm` | `float` | Optional | `30.0` | Transmit RF power in dBm ($30.0 \text{ dBm} = 1.0 \text{ W}$). |

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `scene_name` (`str`): Simulated scene name.
- `frequency_hz` (`float`): Carrier frequency in Hz.
- `tx_position` (`list[float]`): $[x, y, z]$ in meters.
- `rx_position` (`list[float]`): $[x, y, z]$ in meters.
- `tx_power_dbm` (`float`): Transmit power in dBm.
- `backend` (`str`): Active computational backend.
- `num_paths` (`int`): Number of discrete propagation paths resolved.
- `total_path_loss_db` (`float`): Total channel path loss in dB.
- `received_power_dbm` (`float`): Total received power in dBm.
- `min_delay_ns` (`float`): Minimum path arrival delay in nanoseconds.
- `max_delay_ns` (`float`): Maximum path arrival delay in nanoseconds.
- `delays_ns` (`list[float]`): Per-path propagation delays in nanoseconds.
- `path_distances_m` (`list[float]`): Per-path geometric travel distances in meters.
- `path_gains_db` (`list[float]`): Per-path power gains in dB.
- `interaction_types` (`list[str]`): Physical mechanisms per path (e.g., `["Line of Sight (LOS)", "Specular Reflection"]`).

### Possible Errors
- `ToolError`: If `frequency_hz <= 0`, `tx_position` or `rx_position` are not 3-element numeric lists, or the scene is unknown.

---

## 4. `generate_radio_map`

### Purpose
Generate a 2D spatial radio coverage map over a horizontal evaluation plane using Sionna RT's `RadioMapSolver`. Extracts numerical field matrices (linear and dB received power and path gain), renders high-resolution heatmaps, and automatically persists the experiment to disk via `ExperimentStore`.

### Input Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Required? | Default | Description & Constraints |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| `scene_name` | `str` | Optional | `"simple_reflector"` | Geometric scene identifier. |
| `frequency_hz` | `float` | Optional | `3.5e9` | Carrier frequency in Hertz ($> 0.0$ Hz). |
| `tx_name` | `str` | Optional | `"tx"` | Label for the transmitter node. |
| `tx_position` | `list[float]` | Optional | `[0.0, -10.0, 5.0]` | Transmitter 3D position $[x, y, z]$ in meters. |
| `tx_power_dbm` | `float` | Optional | `30.0` | Output RF power in dBm. |
| `tx_orientation_deg`| `list[float]` | Optional | `[0.0, 0.0, 0.0]` | Transmitter $[yaw, pitch, roll]$ in degrees. |
| `antenna_type` | `str` | Optional | `"isotropic"` | Pattern type: `"isotropic"`, `"dipole"`, or `"directional"`. |
| `polarization` | `str` | Optional | `"V"` | Polarization: `"V"`, `"H"`, `"VH"`, or `"cross"`. |
| `num_rows` | `int` | Optional | `1` | Antenna array rows ($\ge 1$). |
| `num_cols` | `int` | Optional | `1` | Antenna array columns ($\ge 1$). |
| `horizontal_spacing`| `float` | Optional | `0.5` | Element spacing in wavelengths ($\lambda$). |
| `vertical_spacing` | `float` | Optional | `0.5` | Element spacing in wavelengths ($\lambda$). |
| `region_mode` | `str` | Optional | `"auto"` | Bounding box mode: `"auto"` or `"manual"`. |
| `auto_margin` | `float` | Optional | `5.0` | Buffer margin in meters added to scene bounds. |
| `auto_include_tx` | `bool` | Optional | `True` | Expand auto bounds to enclose transmitter. |
| `x_min`, `x_max` | `float` | Conditional | `None` | Bounds in meters (required if `region_mode="manual"`). |
| `y_min`, `y_max` | `float` | Conditional | `None` | Bounds in meters (required if `region_mode="manual"`). |
| `receiver_height` | `float` | Optional | `1.5` | Evaluation plane height ($z$) in meters. |
| `cell_size` | `float` or `list` | Optional | `1.0` | Grid resolution in meters: scalar ($1.0$) or $[dx, dy]$. |
| `samples_per_tx` | `int` | Optional | `1_000_000` | Monte Carlo ray budget per transmitter. |
| `max_depth` | `int` | Optional | `3` | Maximum ray bounce depth. |
| `seed` | `int` | Optional | `None` | Random seed for deterministic reproducibility. |
| `los` | `bool` | Optional | `True` | Enable Line of Sight rays. |
| `specular_reflection`| `bool`| Optional | `True` | Enable specular reflections. |
| `diffraction` | `bool` | Optional | `True` | Enable edge diffraction. |
| `scattering` | `bool` | Optional | `False` | Enable diffuse scattering. |
| `experiment_name` | `str` | Optional | `None` | Optional human-readable experiment label. |

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `status` (`str`): `"success"`.
- `experiment_id` (`str`): Unique generated identifier (e.g., `"experiment_20261101_135328_38565204"`).
- `scene_name` (`str`): Evaluated scene.
- `grid_dimensions` (`list[int]`): $[Ny, Nx]$ grid dimensions.
- `total_cells` (`int`): Total evaluation cells ($Ny \times Nx$).
- `valid_cells` (`int`): Cells receiving finite RF power ($> 0.0$ W).
- `zero_power_cells` (`int`): Unreachable cells receiving $0.0$ W.
- `coverage_percentage` (`float`): Percentage of cells reached ($0.0$ to $100.0\%$).
- `min_rss_dbm` (`float`): Minimum finite RSS across the grid.
- `mean_rss_dbm` (`float`): Mean finite RSS across the grid.
- `max_rss_dbm` (`float`): Maximum finite RSS across the grid.
- `artifact_paths` (`dict`):
  - `rss_heatmap` (`str`): Absolute path to saved RSS PNG heatmap.
  - `path_gain_heatmap` (`str`): Absolute path to saved Path Gain PNG heatmap.
  - `metadata_json` (`str`): Absolute path to `metadata.json`.
  - `radio_map_data_npz` (`str`): Absolute path to `radio_map_data.npz`.

### Possible Errors
- `ToolError`: If parameters fail validation (`frequency_hz <= 0`, manual bounds missing when `region_mode="manual"`, invalid antenna type or polarization).
- `ToolError`: If ray-tracing execution fails.

---

## 5. `list_experiments`

### Purpose
Retrieve lightweight summary records for all previously persisted radio-map simulation experiments. This tool reads metadata without loading large NPZ numerical matrices into memory.

### Parameters
*None.* This tool takes no input arguments.

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `status` (`str`): `"success"`.
- `count` (`int`): Total number of stored experiments.
- `experiments` (`list[dict]`): List of lightweight summaries containing:
  - `experiment_id` (`str`): Unique ID.
  - `experiment_name` (`str` or `null`): User-specified label.
  - `created_at` (`str`): ISO 8601 UTC creation timestamp.
  - `scene_name` (`str`): Scene identifier.
  - `frequency_hz` (`float`): Carrier frequency in Hz.
  - `measurement_height` (`float`): Height in meters.
  - `grid_dimensions` (`list[int]`): $[Ny, Nx]$.
  - `coverage_percentage` (`float`): Coverage percentage.
  - `mean_rss_dbm` (`float`): Mean received power.
  - `heatmaps_available` (`dict`): Boolean flags for RSS and Path Gain heatmaps.

### Sample Response
```json
{
  "status": "success",
  "count": 2,
  "experiments": [
    {
      "experiment_id": "experiment_20261101_135328_38565204",
      "experiment_name": "validation_run_30dbm",
      "created_at": "2026-11-01T13:53:28.159443+00:00",
      "scene_name": "simple_reflector",
      "frequency_hz": 3500000000.0,
      "measurement_height": 1.5,
      "grid_dimensions": [5, 3],
      "coverage_percentage": 100.0,
      "mean_rss_dbm": -32.09,
      "heatmaps_available": {
        "rss_heatmap": true,
        "path_gain_heatmap": true
      }
    }
  ]
}
```

---

## 6. `get_experiment_details`

### Purpose
Retrieve complete configuration metadata, antenna specifications, summary metrics, and absolute artifact paths for a specific saved experiment.

### Input Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Required? | Constraints & Description |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| `experiment_id` | `str` | **Required** | Unique identifier (e.g. `"experiment_20261101_135328_38565204"`). |

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `status` (`str`): `"success"`.
- `experiment_id` (`str`): Experiment identifier.
- `experiment_name` (`str` or `null`): Label.
- `scene_name` (`str`): Scene name.
- `frequency_hz` (`float`): Frequency in Hz.
- `tx_position` (`list[float]`): $[x, y, z]$ coordinates.
- `tx_power_dbm` (`float`): Transmit power.
- `tx_orientation_deg` (`list[float]`): $[yaw, pitch, roll]$.
- `antenna_type` (`str`), `polarization` (`str`): Antenna settings.
- `measurement_height` (`float`): Plane height.
- `region_bounds` (`list[float]`): $[x_{\min}, x_{\max}, y_{\min}, y_{\max}]$.
- `cell_size` (`list[float]`): $[dx, dy]$.
- `grid_dimensions` (`list[int]`): $[Ny, Nx]$.
- `solver_parameters` (`dict`): Ray tracing parameters.
- `summary_metrics` (`dict`): Min, mean, max RSS, cell counts, coverage %.
- `artifact_paths` (`dict`): Absolute paths to PNGs, NPZ, and JSON.

### Possible Errors
- `ToolError`: If `experiment_id` is empty, contains path traversal characters, or the experiment does not exist (`FileNotFoundError`).

---

## 7. `compare_experiments`

### Purpose
Compare two saved radio-map simulation experiments across identical spatial geometries, evaluating received power difference and path-gain difference. Renders publication-quality centered diverging difference heatmaps.

### Comparison Convention
All differences strictly adhere to the **$B - A$ (Experiment B minus Experiment A)** convention:
$$\Delta \text{RSS}_{\text{dBm}} = \text{RSS}_B - \text{RSS}_A$$
$$\Delta \text{PathGain}_{\text{dB}} = \text{PathGain}_B - \text{PathGain}_A$$
- **Positive ($> 0$ dB):** Experiment B is stronger / has higher gain.
- **Negative ($< 0$ dB):** Experiment B is weaker / has lower gain.
- **Zero ($== 0$ dB):** Both experiments are physically identical.

### Input Parameters

| Parameter | Type | Required? | Default | Description |
| :--- | :---: | :---: | :---: | :--- |
| `experiment_id_a` | `str` | **Required** | — | Identifier of baseline/reference Experiment A. |
| `experiment_id_b` | `str` | **Required** | — | Identifier of modified Experiment B. |
| `generate_heatmaps` | `bool` | Optional | `True` | Whether to render difference PNG heatmaps. |

### Return Schema (`dict`)
- `status` (`str`): `"success"`.
- `difference_convention` (`str`): `"B - A (Experiment B minus Experiment A)"`.
- `experiment_a_id`, `experiment_b_id` (`str`): Compared IDs.
- `scene_name` (`str`): Scene name.
- `grid_dimensions` (`list[int]`): $[Ny, Nx]$.
- `total_cells` (`int`): Total cells evaluated.
- `valid_cells` (`int`): Cells where both A and B have finite power.
- `invalid_cells` (`int`): Cells unreached in either A or B (excluded from statistics).
- `rss_difference` (`dict`):
  - `mean_db` (`float`): Mean difference $\mu_\Delta$.
  - `min_db` (`float`): Minimum difference.
  - `max_db` (`float`): Maximum difference.
  - `std_db` (`float`): Standard deviation $\sigma_\Delta$.
- `path_gain_difference` (`dict`):
  - `mean_db` (`float`): Mean path gain difference.
  - `min_db`, `max_db`, `std_db` (`float`): Range and variance.
- `artifact_paths` (`dict`):
  - `rss_difference_heatmap` (`str` or `null`): Path to RSS difference PNG.
  - `path_gain_difference_heatmap` (`str` or `null`): Path to Path Gain difference PNG.

### Possible Errors
- `ToolError`: If either experiment ID is missing or not found on disk.
- `ToolError`: If experiments are spatially incompatible (different scenes, heights, grid dimensions, cell sizes, or misaligned coordinates).
</file>

